<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; History by century</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: The 4th century BCE</h1><p class="ws-lede">A worksheet on the 300s BCE: the states, events and people of the century. Use its page to find the answers.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">Open the century page &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Name the states on the map</h2><p class="ws-how">Each number marks the capital or seat of a state that existed in 350 BCE. Write its name from the word bank on the line with the same number.</p><figure class="ws-mapfig"><svg class="map map-world ws-map" viewBox="0 0 10519 5536" role="img" aria-label="Map: 7 numbered states of the world in 350 BCE"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><g class="ws-pins"><g><circle cx="5259" cy="1106" r="179"/><text x="5259" y="1181" font-size="215">1</text></g><g><circle cx="7909" cy="1324" r="179"/><text x="7909" y="1399" font-size="215">2</text></g><g><circle cx="6140" cy="1251" r="179"/><text x="6140" y="1326" font-size="215">3</text></g><g><circle cx="5866" cy="834" r="179"/><text x="5866" y="909" font-size="215">4</text></g><g><circle cx="6237" cy="1859" r="179"/><text x="6237" y="1934" font-size="215">5</text></g><g><circle cx="7266" cy="1513" r="179"/><text x="7266" y="1588" font-size="215">6</text></g><g><circle cx="7222" cy="2122" r="179"/><text x="7222" y="2197" font-size="215">7</text></g></g></svg><figcaption>The world in 350 BCE</figcaption></figure><p class="ws-bank"><b>Word bank:</b> State of Chu &middot; Kingdom of Magadha &middot; Bosporan Kingdom &middot; Punic Empire &middot; Anuradhapura Kingdom &middot; Achaemenid Empire &middot; Kingdom of Saba</p><ol class="ws-blanks"><li><span class="ws-line1"></span></li><li><span class="ws-line1"></span></li><li><span class="ws-line1"></span></li><li><span class="ws-line1"></span></li><li><span class="ws-line1"></span></li><li><span class="ws-line1"></span></li><li><span class="ws-line1"></span></li></ol></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Put the events in order</h2><p class="ws-how">Number the events from 1 (earliest) to 6 (latest).</p><ul class="ws-order"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Peace of Antalcidas</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Corinthian War</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Battle of Leuctra</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Battle of Ipsus</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Battle of Gaugamela</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Battle of Chaeronea (338 BCE)</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Match the people to their dates</h2><p class="ws-how">Write the number of the right dates next to each person. Look them up in the encyclopedia.</p><div class="ws-match"><ol class="ws-ma" type="A"><li>Aristotle</li><li>Antigonus I Monophthalmus</li><li>Lysimachus</li><li>Plato</li><li>Ptolemy I Soter</li><li>Mencius</li></ol><ol class="ws-mb"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>c. 360–281 BCE</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>c. 428–348 BCE</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>c. 367–282 BCE</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>c. 372–289 BCE</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>384–322 BCE</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>382–301 BCE</li></ol></div></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">4</span>Key terms</h2><p class="ws-how">These words appear in the entries of this century. Explain each one in your own words.</p><ul class="ws-terms"><li><b>Catapult</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Phalanx</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Sarissa</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Siege tower</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>War elephant</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">5</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>In the 300s BCE, 133 recorded states were founded and 83 came to an end. Choose one of each from the century page. What happened to them?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The great powers of the age included the Punic Empire, the Roman Republic and the State of Chu. Choose one: what made it strong in these hundred years, and what threatened it?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Choose one event from the list and one person. How might they be connected? Use the encyclopedia to find out.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>If you could visit one place in the 4th century BCE, where would you go, and what would you hope to see?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/centuries/4th-century-bce. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: The 4th century BCE</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Name the states on the map</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>Punic Empire <span>814 BCE – 146 BCE</span></li><li>State of Chu <span>1030 BCE – 223 BCE</span></li><li>Achaemenid Empire <span>559 BCE – 330 BCE</span></li><li>Bosporan Kingdom <span>438 BCE – 370 CE</span></li><li>Kingdom of Saba <span>800 BCE – 275 CE</span></li><li>Kingdom of Magadha <span>600 BCE – 345 BCE</span></li><li>Anuradhapura Kingdom <span>437 BCE – 1017 CE</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Put the events in order</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>Corinthian War <span>395–387 BCE</span></li><li>Peace of Antalcidas <span>387–386 BCE</span></li><li>Battle of Leuctra <span>371 BCE</span></li><li>Battle of Chaeronea (338 BCE) <span>338 BCE</span></li><li>Battle of Gaugamela <span>331 BCE</span></li><li>Battle of Ipsus <span>301 BCE</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Match the people to their dates</h2><ol class="ws-key-list" type="A"><li>Aristotle <span>5: 384–322 BCE</span></li><li>Antigonus I Monophthalmus <span>6: 382–301 BCE</span></li><li>Lysimachus <span>1: c. 360–281 BCE</span></li><li>Plato <span>2: c. 428–348 BCE</span></li><li>Ptolemy I Soter <span>3: c. 367–282 BCE</span></li><li>Mencius <span>4: c. 372–289 BCE</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">4</span>Key terms</h2><dl class="ws-key-terms"><dt>Catapult</dt><dd>A catapult is a siege engine that hurls projectiles using tension, torsion or human traction, used before gunpowder artillery.</dd><dt>Phalanx</dt><dd>A phalanx is a dense formation of heavily armed infantry fighting in close ranks.</dd><dt>Sarissa</dt><dd>The sarissa was a very long pike, several metres in length, carried by the infantry of the Macedonian phalanx.</dd><dt>Siege tower</dt><dd>A siege tower is a mobile wooden tower used to bring attackers to the top of fortification walls.</dd><dt>War elephant</dt><dd>A war elephant is an elephant trained for combat, used to charge enemy formations, carry archers and intimidate opponents.</dd></dl></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">5</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers name states, people and events from the century page.</p></section></div></details>
